---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import GuideData from "../../components/GuideData.astro";
import { bySlug, kicker } from "../../dossiers/registry.js";
import { dateStamp } from "../../dossiers/display.js";
const D = bySlug["gender"];
---
<Base
  title="Women & Men · India Atlas"
  description={D.description}
  nav="guides"
  dateStamp={dateStamp(D)}
  crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / ${D.section} / <b>${D.crumbTitle}</b>`}
  crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`}
  canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/gender/"
  ogImage={`/og/${D.slug}.png`}
>
<div class="page">

  <div class="titleblock break">
    <div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div>
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <h1 set:html={D.headline} />
      <p class="lede">The sex ratio, seven census rounds long, tells one story: how many women survive to be counted. The rest of this guide asks what happens to them after: whether they live as long, learn as much, work as much, and are as safe. Every indicator draws on a different source; none is spliced into the next.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Source <b>MoSPI, multi-source compendium</b></span>
      <span>Sources <b>Census, SRS, NFHS, PLFS, NCRB</b></span>
      <span>Coverage <b>Demography, health, education, work, safety</b></span>
      <span>Sex ratio <b>1951 to 2011, 7 census rounds</b></span>
      <span>Latest SRS <b>2020</b></span>
      <span>Latest PLFS <b>2023-24</b></span>
    </div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Sex ratio, 2011, ♀ per 1000 ♂</div><div class="v acc" id="n-sr">–</div><div class="d" id="d-sr">–</div><div class="nspark" id="nspark-sr"></div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Female LFPR, 15+, per cent</div><div class="v" id="n-lfpr">–</div><div class="d" id="d-lfpr">–</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Maternal Mortality Ratio</div><div class="v" id="n-mmr">–</div><div class="d" id="d-mmr">–</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Life expectancy gap, ♀ − ♂, years</div><div class="v" id="n-le">–</div><div class="d" id="d-le">–</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- § 01 current reading -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The current reading</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Latest available, each indicator its own vintage</span></div>
    <div class="reading-top">
      <div><span class="rk">Sex ratio, 2011 census</span><div class="big" id="rb-sr">–</div></div>
      <p class="rt-cap">Females per 1,000 males, the 2011 Census: the latest full count. The ratio recovered from its 1991 nadir of 927 but never returned to the 1951 level of 946. Maternal mortality, the biggest single improvement, fell from <b id="rt-mmr0">–</b> to <b id="rt-mmr1">–</b> deaths per 100,000 live births.</p>
      <div class="rt-side"><span class="rk">MMR, 2018-20</span><div class="rt-side-v" id="rb-mmr">–</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="reading-cols">
      <div class="reading-col">
        <span class="mk">What the print says</span>
        <p>The 2011 Census counted <b id="cr-sr">–</b> females per 1,000 males: up from <b id="cr-sr-nadir">–</b> in 1991, the lowest point on record. Infant mortality in 2020 was equal for boys and girls (<b id="cr-imr">–</b> per thousand), closing a gap that once ran against girls. Life expectancy at birth favours women by <b id="cr-le-gap">–</b> years.</p>
        <p>Maternal mortality fell to <b id="cr-mmr">–</b> per 100,000 live births (2018-20): a <b id="cr-mmr-drop">–</b> decline from 2004-06. Literacy among women (age 7+) reached <b id="cr-lit-f">–</b>, against <b id="cr-lit-m">–</b> for men. And <b id="cr-lfpr-f">–</b> of working-age women were in the labour force, against <b id="cr-lfpr-m">–</b> of men.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="reading-col now">
        <span class="mk">What it does not say</span>
        <p>The sex ratio is a stock count from 2011: fourteen years old. The Sample Registration System carries it forward to 2020, but a census has not been held since, so the ratio's current level is an estimate on top of an estimate.</p>
        <p>Each indicator here is its own source with its own methodology and its own vintage. They are shown together because they describe one subject, the condition of women relative to men, but they were never designed to be one series. Comparing the literacy rate (a census/NSO measure) with the LFPR (a sample survey) across the same year is approximate at best.</p>
        <p>What none of these indicators measures is the <em>quality</em> of survival, education or work. Women living longer does not mean living healthier; enrolling does not mean graduating; being in the labour force does not mean earning equally. The compendium counts positions, not outcomes.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 register -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>The register of series</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Latest, vs previous, since first</span></div>
    <div class="break ledger">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Series</th><th>Latest</th><th>vs previous</th><th>First</th><th class="spark">History</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="ledger-body"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 the balance sheet -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>The balance sheet</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Women vs men, latest available, one scale per metric</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-balance" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Five gaps, five directions</h3>
            <div class="sub">Women and men compared on survival, education and work, latest published, female in blue, male in grey</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-balance">–</div><div class="c">female life expectancy advantage, years</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-balance"></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI compendium: SRS (IMR, life expectancy), Census/NSO (literacy), PLFS (LFPR), each metric on its own scale, the dumbbell shows the gap, not the level</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Five dumbbells, one per metric. The grey dot is men, the blue dot women; the line between them is the gender gap on that measure. Three of the five favour women: they live longer, their IMR has converged to equality, and the literacy gap is narrowing. Two favour men overwhelmingly: the labour-force gap is the widest dumbbell on the page.</p>
            <p>Each dumbbell is on its own scale (IMR is per thousand, literacy and LFPR are per cent, life expectancy is years): read the <em>direction</em> of each gap, not the distance between dumbbells. The point of putting them together is to show that "gender parity" is not one condition but several, each moving at its own speed.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of latest</span>
            <p>Life expectancy: women ahead by <b id="ex-b-le">–</b> years. Literacy: men ahead by <b id="ex-b-lit">–</b> points. LFPR: men ahead by <b id="ex-b-lfpr">–</b> points: the widest gap on the page. IMR: <b id="ex-b-imr">–</b>, equal within rounding.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 headline : sex ratio -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>Plate 1: the seventy-year count</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Sex ratio, females per 1,000 males, census 1951-2011</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-sr" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The line that fell, then climbed halfway back</h3>
            <div class="sub">Sex ratio (females per 1,000 males), All India, seven Census rounds 1951-2011</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-sr">–</div><div class="c">1951 → 2011, the swing</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone">
            <div id="plot-sr" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Census of India, females per 1,000 males, seven rounds, decadal, the 1991 nadir of 927 is the low point of the 'missing women' story; the recovery to 943 by 2011 did not reach the 1951 level of 946, no census has been held since 2011</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Seven points, one per census. The line falls from 946 in 1951 to 927 in 1991: four decades of decline that traced the spread of prenatal sex selection and the persistent neglect of girl children. Then it turns: 933 in 2001, 943 in 2011. The recovery is real but incomplete; the ratio sits three below where it began, sixty years earlier.</p>
            <p>The line stops at 2011 because the next census was not held. The Sample Registration System carries the sex ratio forward in Plate 7, but a survey estimate is not a census count. Treat anything after 2011 as provisional.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2011</span>
            <p>The 2011 ratio: <b id="ex-sr-now">–</b>, up from <b id="ex-sr-nadir">–</b> at the 1991 nadir. The swing from nadir to now: <b id="ex-sr-swing">–</b> points: but still <b id="ex-sr-gap">–</b> below the 1951 starting point.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 05 what this measures -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">05</span><h2>What this measures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>This guide is not one survey. It is the "Women and Men in India" compendium: a MoSPI publication that gathers indicators from a dozen sources and arranges them by sex. The Census provides the sex ratio and literacy; the Sample Registration System (SRS) provides fertility and mortality; NFHS provides health behaviours; PLFS provides labour; NCRB provides crime. Each carries its own methodology, sample frame and vintage.</p>
        <p>The sex ratio, females per 1,000 males, is the simplest and the most contested. It measures survival, not preference: how many girls are born and live to be counted, against how many boys. A ratio below 1,000 means fewer women than men, which in India's case reflects both prenatal sex selection and excess female mortality. The 1991 nadir of 927 was the statistical signature of "missing women."</p>
        <p>The other metrics fill in the life that follows. <b>Infant mortality rate</b> (IMR) is deaths in the first year per 1,000 live births. <b>Life expectancy at birth</b> is years lived, by gender. <b>Maternal mortality ratio</b> (MMR) is maternal deaths per 100,000 live births. <b>Literacy</b> is the per cent age 7+ who can read and write. <b>LFPR</b> is the labour force participation rate: the share working or seeking work. None of these is invented; none is spliced with another.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>No census since 2011.</b> The sex ratio is a 14-year-old stock count. The SRS continues, but a survey estimate is not a census. Everything after 2011 is provisional.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>Each indicator its own source.</b> Comparing literacy (Census/NSO) with LFPR (PLFS) across the same year is approximate: different frames, different methods, different vintages. </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iii</span>
          <b>Positions, not outcomes.</b> Living longer ≠ living healthier. Enrolling ≠ graduating. In the labour force ≠ earning equally. The compendium counts where women stand, not where they arrive.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 06 survival : MMR + IMR -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">06</span><h2>Plate 2: survival</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">MMR &amp; IMR, the mortality story</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-mmr" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The steepest decline in the compendium</h3>
            <div class="sub">Maternal Mortality Ratio, All India, 3-year moving averages 2004-06 to 2018-20</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-mmr">–</div><div class="c">2004-06 → 2018-20, decline</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone">
            <div id="plot-mmr" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: SRS, Office of the Registrar General, maternal deaths per 100,000 live births, overlapping 3-year windows plotted at each period's start year, the decline from 254 to 97 is a 62 per cent drop in 14 years</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>One line, nine points, falling steeply. Maternal mortality, deaths from pregnancy and childbirth per 100,000 live births, dropped from 254 in 2004-06 to 97 in 2018-20. That is the steepest mortality improvement in the compendium, and the clearest development-success story on this page. The rate is now below the SDG "sub-target" pace, though still above the 2030 goal of 70.</p>
            <p>The windows overlap (each is a 3-year moving average), so the line is smoother than an annual series would be. The later points are closer together because the SRS began publishing annually from 2014-16, having previously released at 3-year intervals: the tighter spacing is a publication change, not an acceleration.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2018-20</span>
            <p>MMR: <b id="ex-mmr-now">–</b>, down from <b id="ex-mmr-first">–</b> in 2004-06: a <b id="ex-mmr-drop">–</b> decline. At this pace the SDG 2030 target of 70 is within reach, but the last stretch (rural, poorest states) is the hardest.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-imr" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The gap that closed the wrong way, then righted</h3>
            <div class="sub">Infant Mortality Rate by gender, All India, SRS 2008-2020, male in grey, female in blue</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-imr">–</div><div class="c">2020, female IMR, equal to male</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-imr">
            <button class="chip" data-key="female"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Female IMR</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="male"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Male IMR</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone">
            <div id="plot-imr" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: SRS, infant deaths per 1,000 live births, by gender, biologically, female infants survive better than male; India's female IMR exceeded male for years, the statistical trace of son preference. The lines converged to equality by 2020</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Two lines, both falling: infant mortality halved in twelve years. But look at their order: at the start, female IMR (blue) sat <em>above</em> male. That is the reverse of biology. Everywhere in the world, girl infants are hardier than boys; in India they died more often, the statistical trace of unequal care. By 2020 the lines crossed to equality: not to the female advantage biology predicts, but to parity. The excess female infant death is gone; the biological edge is not yet visible.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2020</span>
            <p>IMR: male <b id="ex-imr-m">–</b>, female <b id="ex-imr-f">–</b>: equal, within rounding. In 2008 the gap was <b id="ex-imr-gap0">–</b> against girls; by 2020 it had closed. Both halved, from the low fifties to <b id="ex-imr-now">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 07 life expectancy -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">07</span><h2>Plate 3: living longer, living apart</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Life expectancy at birth, by gender, 1990-94 to 2031-36</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-le" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Women gain more years than men</h3>
            <div class="sub">Life expectancy at birth, All India, by gender, 5-year cohort periods, observed to 2016-20, projected after (dashed)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-le">–</div><div class="c">female advantage, latest observed</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-le">
            <button class="chip" data-key="female"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Female</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="male"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Male</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone">
            <div id="plot-le" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: SRS, Office of the Registrar General, life expectancy at birth by gender, 5-year cohort periods (e.g. 1990-94), periods after 2016-20 are projections (marked '*', dashed), the female advantage is universal and widening</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Two rising lines: both sexes are living longer. But the female line (blue) sits above the male throughout, and the gap widens with time. Women gained more years from the mortality transition than men did, which is the universal pattern: female biology plus male risk-taking (occupational, tobacco, alcohol, violence) compound over development.</p>
            <p>The line turns dashed after 2016-20: those periods are projections, not observed. The report estimates future life expectancy from current age-specific rates; treat them as informed guesses, not measurements.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2016-20 (latest observed)</span>
            <p>Male life expectancy <b id="ex-le-m">–</b>, female <b id="ex-le-f">–</b>: women ahead by <b id="ex-le-gap">–</b> years. In 1990-94 the gap was <b id="ex-le-gap0">–</b>; it has grown by about a year. Projected to <b id="ex-le-proj">–</b> (F) and <b id="ex-le-projm">–</b> (M) by 2031-36.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 08 fertility -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">08</span><h2>Plate 4: fewer births, by education</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Total Fertility Rate, by education of woman, 2016-2020</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-tfr" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The steepest gradient in the country</h3>
            <div class="sub">Total Fertility Rate by education level of woman, All India, 2016-2020, replacement level at 2.1</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-tfr">–</div><div class="c">graduate TFR, below replacement</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-tfr">
            <button class="chip" data-key="graduate"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Graduate &amp; above</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="illiterate"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Illiterate</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone">
            <div id="plot-tfr" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: SRS, TFR by education level of woman, the gradient is monotone: fertility falls with every step of schooling, graduates are well below the replacement rate of 2.1; illiterate women are above it</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Seven lines, one per education level, all below the national average at the top and above it at the bottom. The gradient is monotone: every additional step of schooling lowers the number of children a woman has. Graduate women are at 1.6: well below the replacement rate of 2.1. Illiterate women are at 3.3: above it.</p>
            <p>This is not a causal claim that education <em>causes</em> lower fertility. Education correlates with urban residence, later marriage, labour-force participation, access to contraception and child survival: each of which lowers fertility on its own. The gradient is the country's single strongest demographic signal, but its drivers are many.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2020</span>
            <p>Graduate TFR: <b id="ex-tfr-grad">–</b>. Illiterate TFR: <b id="ex-tfr-ill">–</b>. The gap: <b id="ex-tfr-gap">–</b> children per woman. India's aggregate TFR has fallen below replacement: the education gradient explains why the average conceals two different countries.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 09 literacy -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">09</span><h2>Plate 5: the literacy gap</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Literacy rate, age 7+, by gender, 1981-2017</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-lit" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Closing, not closed</h3>
            <div class="sub">Literacy rate (age 7+), All India, by gender, Census 1981-2011 + NSO 2017 estimate</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-lit">–</div><div class="c">2017, gender gap, percentage points</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-lit">
            <button class="chip" data-key="female"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Female</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="male"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Male</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone">
            <div id="plot-lit" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Census of India (1981-2011) + NSO 2017 estimate (marked '*'), literacy rate for persons aged 7+ who can read and write, the female line rises faster, narrowing the gap from 27 points (1981) to 14 (2017)</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Two rising lines. Both sexes became more literate over four decades, but the female line (blue) rose faster: from 31 per cent in 1981 to 70 per cent in 2017. The gap narrowed from 27 points to 14. That is real progress, but 14 points is still a chasm: one in seven women who should be literate by the male standard is not.</p>
            <p>The last point (2017) is an NSO estimate, not a census round. A census has not been held since 2011, so the current gap is an estimate of an estimate.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2017 (NSO estimate)</span>
            <p>Male literacy <b id="ex-lit-m">–</b>, female <b id="ex-lit-f">–</b>: a gap of <b id="ex-lit-gap">–</b> points. In 1981 the gap was <b id="ex-lit-gap0">–</b>. At this rate the gap reaches zero around <b id="ex-lit-eta">–</b>: but literacy gaps close slowly at the end, as the hardest-to-reach populations are last.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 10 work -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">10</span><h2>Plate 6: the work gap</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">LFPR, 15+, usual status, by gender, PLFS</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-lfpr" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The widest dumbbell, narrowing at last</h3>
            <div class="sub">Labour Force Participation Rate (15+, usual status ps+ss), All India, by gender, PLFS 2017-18 to 2023-24</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-lfpr">–</div><div class="c">female LFPR, 2023-24, rising</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-lfpr">
            <button class="chip" data-key="female"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Female LFPR</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="male"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Male LFPR</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone">
            <div id="plot-lfpr" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: PLFS, MoSPI/NSO, LFPR for persons aged 15+, usual status (ps+ss), all sectors, agriculture years (Jul–Jun) plotted at the start year, female LFPR rose from 23% to 42% in six years, partly real, partly a measurement-frame effect</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The widest gap on the page. Male LFPR (grey) is flat near 77 per cent throughout; female LFPR (blue) sat below 25 per cent for years, among the lowest in the world for a country at India's income, then rose sharply to 42 per cent. The rise is the most encouraging recent signal in this guide, but it needs reading carefully.</p>
            <p>Part of the rise is real. Part is a measurement effect: PLFS widened its frame and its recall over these years, and the "usual status" definition counts unpaid family work in agriculture and self-employment more completely than earlier surveys did. A woman helping on the family farm for the better part of a year now enters the labour force; whether she did not work before, or was not counted, the survey alone cannot say.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2023-24</span>
            <p>Female LFPR <b id="ex-lfpr-f">–</b>, male <b id="ex-lfpr-m">–</b>: a gap of <b id="ex-lfpr-gap">–</b> points, down from <b id="ex-lfpr-gap0">–</b> in 2017-18. The female line nearly doubled in six years; the male line barely moved. For the measurement caveat, read Guide 06 (PLFS) alongside this one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 11 state sex ratio -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">11</span><h2>Plate 7: where the balance is worst</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Sex ratio by state, SRS, latest</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-stsr" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The north-western deficit</h3>
            <div class="sub">Sex ratio (females per 1,000 males) by state/UT, SRS latest, the blue rule is the All-India ratio</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-stsr">–</div><div class="c">spread: highest minus lowest state</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-stsr"></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: SRS, sex ratio by state/UT, latest published, the deficit concentrates in the north-west (Haryana, Delhi, Punjab), the belt where prenatal sex selection was most widespread, Kerala and the tribal-majority north-east sit above parity</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>One dot per state, sorted low to high, against the All-India rule. The shape is not a slope but a gulf: the north-western states, Haryana, Delhi, Punjab, sit far below the national line, the belt where sex selection hollowed out the ratio for a generation. At the other end, Kerala and the north-eastern tribal-majority states sit at or above parity.</p>
            <p>India does not have one sex ratio; it has two. The north-west runs a deficit of fifty to ninety women per thousand; the south and east do not. A national ratio of 943 is the average of these two different countries, and policy that ignores the geography misses the point.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of latest SRS</span>
            <p>Lowest: <b id="ex-stsr-bot">–</b>. Highest: <b id="ex-stsr-top">–</b>. The spread is <b id="ex-stsr-spread">–</b> points: wider than the entire 70-year swing of the All-India ratio. <b id="ex-stsr-above">–</b> states sit at or above the national line.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 12 how to misread -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">12</span><h2>How to misread this number</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The sex ratio improved from 927 to 943: son preference is over."</h3>
          <p>The national ratio recovered, but the recovery is uneven. The north-western states still run deficits of 50-90 per thousand. A national average conceals the geography: the improvement happened where the deficit was smallest, not where it was worst.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Female LFPR doubled: women are surging into the workforce."</h3>
          <p>Part of the rise is a measurement-frame effect. PLFS widened its coverage and its recall over these years; unpaid family work in agriculture now enters the count more completely. Whether women are working more or being counted better, the survey alone cannot separate. Read Guide 06 alongside this one.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Life expectancy is 74 for women and 71 for men: the gap is biology."</h3>
          <p>Partly. The female advantage is universal, but its <em>size</em> varies with male risk-taking: occupational hazard, tobacco, alcohol, violence. India's gap (3 years) is narrower than in countries where male mortality risks are higher. Biology sets the floor; behaviour sets the ceiling.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iv.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"MMR fell to 97: maternal death is nearly eliminated."</h3>
          <p>97 per 100,000 is still roughly 25,000 women a year. The decline is the steepest in the compendium, but the last stretch, rural, poorest states, the hardest-to-reach, is the slowest. The SDG target is 70 by 2030; reaching it means the ratio must fall another 28 per cent in a decade.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">v.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"These indicators together measure 'women's empowerment'."</h3>
          <p>They measure positions, survival, literacy, labour-force presence, not power. A woman who is literate, alive and counted in the labour force may still earn a third of a man's wage, own no property, and decide nothing at home. The compendium counts where women stand; empowerment is what they can <em>do</em> from there.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">vi.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Each indicator is comparable across the same year."</h3>
          <p>They are not. Each draws on a different source with a different frame: the Census is a complete count, SRS is a sample, NFHS is a household survey, PLFS is a different sample. Putting them on one page is editorial, not statistical: they describe one subject but were never designed to be one series.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

